Step 2: Creating Database Connection
Old JDBC vs Modern Spring Boot (DataSource + Connection Pool)

In any database-driven Java application, the second major step is creating a connection between the application and the database. This connection allows the program to send SQL queries and receive results from the database.

However, the way connections are created has evolved over time. Earlier, developers manually created connections using JDBC. In modern Spring Boot applications, connections are managed automatically using DataSource and connection pooling.

1. Step 2 in Traditional JDBC (Old Approach)
Example Code
Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(url, username, password);
What Happens Internally

When this line executes, the following actions occur:

The Java application contacts the database server.

The database verifies the provided username and password.

A communication channel (connection) is created between the application and the database.

The connection remains active until it is closed manually using con.close().

Key Characteristics

A new connection is created every time the code runs.

Each request requires authentication with the database server.

The developer must manually close the connection.

If the connection is not closed properly, it can cause memory leaks and performance issues.

Problem With This Approach

In small programs, this approach works fine. However, modern applications often have many users accessing the system simultaneously.

Example:

If 1000 users access the application,

The application may try to create 1000 separate database connections.

Creating database connections is expensive in terms of time and system resources. Too many connections can slow down the system or even cause the database to crash.

2. Step 2 in Modern Spring Boot Applications

Modern applications solve this problem using:

DataSource

Connection Pooling (HikariCP)

Spring Boot automatically manages these components, so developers do not need to manually create connections.

What is DataSource?

A DataSource is a Java object responsible for managing database connections.

Instead of creating connections manually, the application asks the DataSource for a connection whenever it needs one.

Responsibilities of DataSource:

Creating database connections

Managing those connections

Providing connections when required

Returning connections back after use

The DataSource is automatically created by Spring Boot using the configuration in the application.properties file.

Example configuration:

spring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/mydb
spring.datasource.username=root
spring.datasource.password=1234
spring.datasource.driver-class-name=com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver

These properties tell Spring Boot:

Which database to connect to

Which user credentials to use

Which JDBC driver should be loaded

Using this information, Spring Boot creates a DataSource object internally.

What is Connection Pooling?

Connection pooling is a performance optimization technique.

Instead of creating a new connection every time, the system creates a group of reusable connections at application startup.

This group is called a connection pool.

Spring Boot uses HikariCP as the default connection pool.

How Connection Pool Works

When the application starts, a fixed number of database connections are created.

These connections are stored in a pool.

When the application needs a connection, it takes one from the pool.

After the task is completed, the connection is returned to the pool.

The same connection can then be reused by another request.

Example:

If the pool size is 10 connections:

Only 10 connections exist.

Multiple users reuse those connections instead of creating new ones.

Internal Flow in Spring Boot

When a Spring Boot application starts, the following process occurs:

Application Starts
       ↓
Spring Boot Reads application.properties
       ↓
Creates DataSource Object
       ↓
Initializes HikariCP Connection Pool
       ↓
Creates Multiple Database Connections
       ↓
Connections Stored in Pool
       ↓
Application Requests Connection When Needed
       ↓
Connection Returned to Pool After Use
Why Modern Applications Use Connection Pooling

Connection pooling provides several advantages:

Improved Performance

Creating a connection takes time. Reusing connections is much faster.

Better Resource Management

Connections are reused instead of repeatedly created and destroyed.

Scalability

The system can handle many users without creating excessive connections.

Automatic Management

Spring Boot automatically manages connection creation, reuse, and closing.

Real-Life Example

Imagine a swimming pool.

Old Approach (DriverManager)

Every time someone wants to swim:

A new swimming pool is built.

After swimming, the pool is destroyed.

This wastes time and resources.

Modern Approach (Connection Pool)

There is already a large swimming pool.

People enter the pool.

Swim.

Leave.

The same pool is used by the next person.

This is much faster and more efficient.